      "math_academy_id": "q-14770",
      "knowledge_point_id": "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function",
      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10$ and the point $P(0,10)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ determine the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(10,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,10).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+4x+10 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,10)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(10,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(10) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(10))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
